WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:07.080
Space travel is the most exciting and challenging adventure humanity has ever undertaken.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.480
But in an irony 
of history,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.340
we may stop ourselves from going 
into space the more we do it.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.580
With every 
rocket launched

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.400
and with every 
satellite deployed,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:23.340
we're creating a trap for ourselves that gets 
deadlier and more dangerous every year.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.300
If it's ever activated,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.960
it could end the Space Age

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.300
and trap us 
on our planet for decades,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.300
or even centuries.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.980
Getting something into space 
is incredibly hard.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.540
To do so, you need 
to move very, very fast.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.640
At first, straight up
to leave the atmosphere,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.700
then sideways to begin 
a sort of circling around the Earth,

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.700
still, very, very fast.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.340
If you do that successfully 
you can enter a Low Earth orbit.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.200
And once in orbit,

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.120
it's very hard 
to get out of orbit.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.480
Unless you have 
energy to spare,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.780
you're sort of locked in here, 
falling around the Earth forever.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.000
That's great for things 
we want to stay up,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.760
like space stations 
and satellites.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.620
And so we moved the majority of humanity's 
space infrastructure to this place,

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.000
just a few hundred kilometers 
above the surface.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.560
Just high enough

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.780
so that the atmosphere 
is so thin,

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.160
that orbiting things 
can stay up for centuries

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.460
before air resistance can slow them 
enough to bring them back to Earth.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.420
But this is also the source 
of our deadly trap.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.380
Rockets are really metal cylinders 
that keep big parts of fuel in place.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.160
Whenever a portion 
of the fuel has been spent,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.000
the empty tanks are dropped 
to make the rocket lighter.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.240
Some parts 
crash down to earth

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.680
or burn up 
in the atmosphere.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.840
But most of the useless 
rocket parts stay up

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.440
and begin 
to orbit the planet.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.040
After decades 
of space travel

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.100
low Earth orbit

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.320
is a junkyard of spent boosters,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.440
broken satellites and millions 
of pieces of shrapnel

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.540
from missile tests 
and explosions.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.760
Right now we know of around 
2,600 defunct satellites,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.880
10,000 objects bigger 
than a monitor,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.420
20,000 as large 
as an apple,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.900
500,000 pieces 
the size of a marble

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.740
and at least 100 million parts 
so small they can't be tracked.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:25.120
This debris is moving 
at speeds of up to 30,000 km/h,

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.420
circling Earth on criss-
crossing orbits multiple times a day.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.439
Orbital speeds are so fast 
that being hit by debris

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.960
the size of a pea is like 
being shot by a plasma gun.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.240
On impact the debris

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.160
vaporises, releasing enough energy 
to punch holes straight through solid metal.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.870
So, we've covered the space around our planet with millions of deadly pieces of destruction,

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.840
and we also put a trillion dollar 
global infrastructure network

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.939
right in the danger zone.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:56.220
It performs critical duties 
essential to the modern world:

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.549
global communication,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.370
GPS 
and navigation,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.390
collecting weather data,

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.040
looking out 
for asteroids

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.669
and all manner 
of scientific discoveries:

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.420
things we would miss very much 
if they suddenly went away.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.680
If just one pea-sized bullet 
hits one of our

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:18.340
1,100 working satellites, 
it will be destroyed instantly.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:23.079
Three or four satellites are already 
being destroyed this way every year.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.040
As the number of satellites and the amount of junk in orbit is expected to grow tenfold in the next decade,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.040
we're approaching 
a tipping point.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.780
But the worst thing in space 
is not tiny pieces of junk.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.080
The worst thing would be 
an unstoppable chain reaction

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.430
that turns a lot of non junk things 
into junk. For example:

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.420
if two satellites hit each other 
in just the right way.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.640
If satellites collide

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.800
they don't stop 
and fall out of the sky.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.260
It's more of a splash 
than a crash.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.340
Orbital speeds 
are so fast

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.340
solid pieces spray 
right through each other,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.560
transforming the two satellites 
into clouds of thousands of little things

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.940
still fast enough 
to destroy more satellites.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:09.640
This could trigger the slowest 
and most destructive sort of domino effect:

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.029
a collision cascade.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.740
Like a shotgun spray,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.649
each collision 
creates more bullets.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.300
What was once 
a single tiny target

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.100
very unlikely to hit anything becomes 
a wall of destruction

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.540
hungry 
to make more.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.520
As more and more satellites 
are destroyed

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.960
the destruction 
accelerates exponentially,

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.460
eventually destroying 
everything parked in orbit.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.180
But space 
is very empty,

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.040
so the first few collisions 
may take a long time.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.100
By the time we realize 
what's happening, it's too late.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.060
One year one satellite 
is destroyed

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.900
and that's 
no big deal.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.440
The next year, five.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.300
The year after, 50.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.280
Until there's nothing left.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.940
The situation in orbit 
is rapidly worsening

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.400
and we may already 
be past the point of no return.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.600
Within 10 years space around Earth may no longer 
be viable for long term satellites or rockets.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.140
The worst case scenario 
is horrifying.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.420
A debris field made of hundreds 
of millions of pieces,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.620
many too small to track,
moving at 30,000 km/h.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.880
It would effectively create 
a deadly barrier around Earth, possibly too dangerous 
to cross.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.480
Dreams 
of moon bases,

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.900
Mars colonies 
or space travel at all

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.680
may be 
set back centuries.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.380
And the loss 
of our space infrastructure

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:33.620
would send some of the technology 
we rely on daily back to the 1970s.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.799
But it might not be too late 
to clean up our mess.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.800
While the space industry has become 
better at avoiding space junk,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.620
it's still growing fast and occasional 
weapon tests don't help.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.820
So there have been a couple of wild 
but also serious suggestions.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.500
About how to quickly remove 
as much deadly space junk as possible

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.540
without creating more 
in the process.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.220
Lots of ideas 
are being thrown around,

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.620
and some 
of the most seriously considered

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.120
involve capture 
and return missions,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.440
which are 
being tested now.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:09.140
One method involves meeting a piece of junk
in orbit with a small satellite and loaded with a net.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:10.600
Once caught,

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.280
a small rocket could be used 
to bring it down towards Earth.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.280
Targets too large for a net might be instead caught 
with a harpoon on a tether.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.540
Instead of firing 
a rocket

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.940
the cleaner would deploy a large sail 
to produce atmospheric drag

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.940
and accelerate
orbital decay.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.260
And there are lots of other wild sci-fi 
sounding proposals too.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.220
Some might use 
giant electromagnets.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.380
These magnetic tugs

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:39.060
work by pushing on 
the magnetic components inside satellites

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.720
that they use 
to stabilize

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.320
and orientate themselves 
in Earth's magnetic field.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.820
These may be safer and more reliable 
than nets and harpoons

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.480
because they never have to make contact 
with the junk they're handling,

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:55.100
so there's no risk of accidentally breaking up 
their target into more junk.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.020
As for the tiniest bits of junk,

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.840
lasers might be the key 
to vaporising them entirely.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.640
Satellites with lasers wouldn't 
need to visit their targets.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.320
They can shoot them 
from far away.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.280
Large objects 
can't exactly be shot down,

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.880
but lasers 
can be used to ablate them,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.640
or burn tiny amounts 
of material off the side

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.400
to push the junk 
to a safer orbit.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:21.660
Whatever technology we use at the end,
we better start doing something soon,

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:27.400
before 100 million bullets become 
a trillion and the trap is set.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:28.720
If we don't act,

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.420
our adventure 
in space

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.480
might end before 
it's even begun.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.740
If our days of dreaming about space exploration 
might be numbered anyway

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:41.100
we better put them 
to good use.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:45.440
One of the things we most like to spend our time on 
is learning more about our universe.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.500
And to do that

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.080
you can just keep watching.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.660
Kurzgesagt and Brilliant 
are collaborating on a six-part video series

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.060
about our favorite science 
and space topics.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.100
Kurzgesagt has worked 
with Brilliant for a bit.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.100
And we love how they teach you science 
and maths in a practical way.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.760
By guiding you through problems 
step by step.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:05.140
So you can actually understand 
the concepts behind them.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:06.540
And maybe one day

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.040
use your knowledge 
on problems like space junk.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.900
Or at least your science projects to start with.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:14.540
If you'd like even more edutainment.

